        // Data source: KODAK PROFESSIONAL TRI-X 320 and 400 Films, publication F-4017 (2016), Eastman Kodak Company
        // Data source: KODAK F-5, «Technical Information -- KODAK Professional Black-and-White Films», AUGUST 1979, (c) Eastman Kodak Company 1979 -- PDF/PROFILES/KODAK/kodak-professional-b-w-film/ (88-page JPG scan set, JPG n = DS n-32), read visually. PROVENANCE CLASS: vendor-measured TYPICAL data. DS 1 states the curves and data "are averages of a number of production coatings and, therefore, do not apply directly to a particular box or roll of film. They do not represent standards or specifications which must be met by Eastman Kodak Company." DS 1 also states that for black-and-white films "the ISO speeds are identical to the ANSI speeds". DS 18: ISO 400/27 deg; 'fast, wide-latitude panchromatic film with a MEDIUM-TOED curve'; grain 'Fine'; resolving 100 high (class 'High') / 50 low. Speed matches the held profile exactly -- resolving power gap fill
        // Data source: BBC Research Department Report No. T-101, 1963/5, and BBC Engineering Division Monograph No. 54, August 1964 -- PDF/PROFILES/ILFORD/1963-05.pdf and «AN ANALYSIS OF FILM GRANULARITY.pdf». ⚠ THIRD-PARTY (method rule 14), image-only scans. ADDED 2026-08-23; full reading on ILFORD_HPS. ⚠ READ THE STOCK IDENTITY BEFORE THE NUMBERS: T-101 measures "Tri-X Type 5223", the 35 mm CINE NEGATIVE, while this profile is the STILL film. Same family and same trade name, not the same product, so everything below is a family datum for this profile and a full one only for a 5223 profile. ⚠ THAT PROFILE NOW EXISTS: EASTMAN_TRI_X_5223 was added 2026-08-24 (queue item C26) and OWNS these numbers -- speed 250/320, gamma 0.64, equivalent grain diameter 2.2 um, Wiener 0.555 square microns, and the Fig. 25 Callier measurement. They are left below as well because they are what this profile's own family reasoning rests on, but EASTMAN_TRI_X_5223 is the citation of record for the 5223 emulsion from here on. WHAT THEY PRINT: T-101 Table 1 p27, manufacturers' data -- 250 A.S.A. tungsten / 320 daylight, "an extremely high speed panchromatic negative material", stored-field telefilm recording; Table 2 p28 -- process control gamma 0.64, equivalent grain diameter 2.2 um; Table 4 p38 -- relative granularity 3.5 (5302 = 1). Monograph 54 Table I p12 -- 250 A.S.A. / 35 deg B.S., Wiener spectrum 0.555 square microns at D 0.48 above base, converting to sigma*1000 = 17.5 against the 17.0 stored at net 1.0. T-101 Table 3 p35 measures the DEVELOPMENT-GAMMA dependence directly: at mean density 0.23 the equivalent diameter is 2.40 um at development gamma 0.56 and 2.72 at 0.94, and at mean density 0.54 it is 2.12 and 2.64 -- so grain diameter scales as sqrt(point gamma) to about 5 %, and FALLS as density rises at fixed development. ⚠ AND T-101 Fig. 25 p37 IS THE CALLIER MEASUREMENT THIS PROJECT HAD RECORDED AS NON-EXISTENT (queue C22): log10 of the Callier quotient against diffuse density for this emulsion at two development gammas, running 0.37 at D 0.1 down to 0.30 at D 1.0, i.e. Q from 2.34 to 2.00, with the specular collection angle stated as 0.0016 steradian. That angle is nearly collimated, so 2.0-2.34 is the UPPER BOUND a directional reader can see and is not a replacement for the 1.3 class value, which corresponds to a real condenser cone -- the measurement supports C22's film x geometry split rather than collapsing it. It also shows Q FALLING with density and varying with development gamma, neither of which the constant-Q model expresses. Recorded; callier_q unchanged⚠ THIRD-PARTY, NON-MANUFACTURER, NOT A MEASUREMENT [T3]. FilmLab Pro v2.1 «published data» browser emulator, https://filmlabpro.com/published-data, engine values read out of its application bundle /assets/index-DdvumSO0.js and archived verbatim in doc/thirdparty/filmlabpro_harvest_2026-08-27.json (harvested 2026-08-27). USED FOR ONE PARAMETER ONLY on this profile -- halation gain and threshold, which were previously at the schema default 0/0/0 with Feature.HALATION unset. The site claims its numbers are digitized from manufacturer publications but names NO instrument, operator, date or laboratory, and its rms granularity contradicts the very datasheets it cites wherever this project holds the same document (Portra 400 6.5 vs E-4050's 4, Acros 100 4.5 vs Fuji's 7, Velvia 50 3.8 vs Fuji's 9, Kodachrome 64 6 vs Kodak's 10). Treat as a reconstruction, never as manufacturer data. Full assessment: doc/NotFound.md §7.1.
